1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,939
A school orders fee software, and nobody notices that it ignores the sibling discount.

2
00:00:05,939 --> 00:00:11,134
Fixed while the requirements are being written, the mistake costs about two thousand rupees.

3
00:00:11,134 --> 00:00:13,791
Found in testing, twenty times that.

4
00:00:13,791 --> 00:00:18,293
Found after the bills have gone out, a hundred times: two lakh rupees.

5
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:21,666
The earlier a mistake is caught, the cheaper it is.

6
00:00:21,666 --> 00:00:27,838
That one fact explains why software is built through careful processes and models.

7
00:00:27,838 --> 00:00:28,996
This unit is about them.

8
00:00:34,467 --> 00:00:39,926
Welcome to Class 12 Computer Science, Unit 6: Software Process Model.

9
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:43,382
Six parts.

10
00:00:43,382 --> 00:00:46,494
Software projects and the development process.

11
00:00:46,494 --> 00:00:49,515
The SDLC, phase by phase.

12
00:00:49,515 --> 00:00:52,158
Analysts, requirements and design.

13
00:00:52,158 --> 00:00:54,125
Data flow diagrams.

14
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:55,622
Software quality.

15
00:00:55,622 --> 00:01:01,440
And the development models: waterfall, prototype, incremental, spiral and agile.

16
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:10,229
Software is programs plus data plus documentation.

17
00:01:10,229 --> 00:01:17,403
A software project is a temporary, planned effort with a fixed goal, a budget, a deadline and a team.

18
00:01:17,403 --> 00:01:22,963
It is managed along four constraints: scope, time, cost and quality.

19
00:01:22,963 --> 00:01:28,247
Pull on one and the others move: add features, and it costs more or takes longer.

20
00:01:29,467 --> 00:01:30,680
Estimating a project.

21
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:34,282
Eight developers for five months is forty person months.

22
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:40,575
Forty person months at sixty thousand is twenty four lakh; with hardware, twenty seven lakh.

23
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,182
Add twenty per cent overhead: thirty two lakh forty thousand.

24
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:53,946
And effort is not time: forty people for one month would not work, because they would have seven hundred and eighty lines of communication between them.

25
00:01:55,967 --> 00:02:01,255
A software development process turns a customer's need into working software.

26
00:02:01,255 --> 00:02:12,349
Every process has four activities: specify what it must do, design and build it, validate that it does what was wanted, and evolve it as needs change.

27
00:02:12,349 --> 00:02:15,969
A process model arranges those activities in time.

28
00:02:15,969 --> 00:02:18,556
Do not mix the three terms up in the exam.

29
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:32,085
The system development life cycle is the sequence of phases every system passes through, each producing a deliverable for the next.

30
00:02:32,085 --> 00:02:35,392
Preliminary investigation defines the problem.

31
00:02:35,392 --> 00:02:42,202
A feasibility study asks whether it is technically, economically, operationally and legally possible.

32
00:02:42,202 --> 00:02:45,092
Analysis gathers the requirements.

33
00:02:45,092 --> 00:02:46,681
Design decides how.

34
00:02:46,681 --> 00:02:52,979
Then coding, testing, implementation with training, and maintenance, which feeds back into the cycle.

35
00:02:53,200 --> 00:03:06,979
Maintenance lasts longest and costs most, sixty to seventy per cent of a system's lifetime cost: fixing defects, adapting to change, adding features, and restructuring to make future change cheaper.

36
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:16,041
The four kinds of feasibility are worth memorising: technical, economic, operational, and schedule or legal.

37
00:03:20,833 --> 00:03:23,413
Two roles look in opposite directions.

38
00:03:23,413 --> 00:03:29,845
The system analyst faces the customer: studies the business problem and defines what the system must do.

39
00:03:29,845 --> 00:03:36,030
The software engineer faces the machine: designs, builds and tests the software that does it.

40
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:39,417
The requirement specification is the contract between them.

41
00:03:40,633 --> 00:03:42,458
How are requirements collected?

42
00:03:42,458 --> 00:03:44,944
Interviews go deep but are slow.

43
00:03:44,944 --> 00:03:47,575
Questionnaires reach many people cheaply.

44
00:03:47,575 --> 00:03:50,400
Observation shows what people really do.

45
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,705
Documents show the real data.

46
00:03:52,705 --> 00:03:59,385
And prototypes let users react to a screen, which they are much better at than describing what they want.

47
00:03:59,385 --> 00:04:10,360
The results go into the SRS, where every requirement must be testable: the system should be fast is useless; a receipt shall print within five seconds can be checked.

48
00:04:11,567 --> 00:04:14,615
Design turns the requirements into a blueprint.

49
00:04:14,615 --> 00:04:19,980
Logical design says what the system does, with diagrams that ignore the machine.

50
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:25,123
Physical design says how: tables, files, screens and modules.

51
00:04:25,123 --> 00:04:34,704
Two words judge a design: high cohesion, each module doing one job, and low coupling, modules depending on each other as little as possible.

52
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,668
A data flow diagram shows how data moves through a system.

53
00:04:43,668 --> 00:04:44,983
Four symbols.

54
00:04:44,983 --> 00:04:49,580
A circle is a process that transforms data, named with a verb.

55
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:53,785
A rectangle is an external entity outside the system.

56
00:04:53,785 --> 00:04:57,913
An open box is a data store, a file or table.

57
00:04:57,913 --> 00:05:01,963
And an arrow is a flow of data, labelled with a noun.

58
00:05:01,963 --> 00:05:07,179
It never shows control flow, timing or decisions: that would be a flowchart.

59
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,435
A level one diagram for result processing.

60
00:05:11,435 --> 00:05:18,297
The subject teacher sends marks to process one, record marks, which stores them in the marks file.

61
00:05:18,297 --> 00:05:26,357
Process two computes the result from the marks and the student file, and process three prints the mark sheet for the student.

62
00:05:26,357 --> 00:05:30,316
Each process is numbered, each arrow names its data.

63
00:05:30,316 --> 00:05:37,584
And data never jumps from one store to another, or from an entity to a store, without passing through a process.

64
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:44,401
Database design converts the ER diagram into tables with three rules.

65
00:05:44,401 --> 00:05:46,562
Every entity becomes a table.

66
00:05:46,562 --> 00:05:53,333
A one to many relationship puts the key of the one side into the many table as a foreign key.

67
00:05:53,333 --> 00:06:01,407
And a many to many relationship becomes a new table, keyed by the pair of foreign keys: student enrols in course.

